            <!-- SECTION 7: RISKS & SAFETY -->
            <h2>7. Understanding Risks & How to Minimize Them</h2>

            <p>Like all surgical procedures, plastic surgery carries inherent risks. Understanding these risks and choosing the right facility and surgeon significantly reduces complications.</p>

            <div class="risk-box">
                <h4>🔴 General Surgical Risks</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Infection:</strong> Risk is 1-2% at accredited facilities with proper sterile technique. Symptoms include fever, increasing redness, discharge.</li>
                    <li><strong>Bleeding/Hematoma:</strong> Most common in first 24-48 hours. May require surgical drainage.</li>
                    <li><strong>Adverse anesthesia reactions:</strong> Rare (<0.5%) with modern anesthetics and proper pre-operative assessment.</li>
                    <li><strong>Blood clots (DVT/PE):</strong> Risk increases with prolonged immobility. Early mobilization and compression devices help prevent.</li>
                    <li><strong>Poor wound healing:</strong> More common in smokers, diabetics, and those with poor nutrition.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="risk-box">
                <h4>🟠 Procedure-Specific Risks</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Rhinoplasty:</strong> Breathing difficulty, asymmetry, over-reduction requiring revision, anosmia (temporary)</li>
                    <li><strong>Breast Augmentation:</strong> Capsular contracture (hardening), implant rupture, change in nipple sensation, asymmetry</li>
                    <li><strong>Liposuction:</strong> Contour irregularities, seroma (fluid collection), skin dimpling or laxity</li>
                    <li><strong>Facelift:</strong> Facial nerve injury (rare, often temporary), hematoma, hair loss near incisions, ear lobe distortion</li>
                    <li><strong>Double Eyelid:</strong> Asymmetry, incomplete crease formation, difficulty closing eyes (temporary)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="procedure-card">
                <h3>How to Minimize Your Risks</h3>
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Choose JCI-accredited facilities:</strong> These meet international safety standards</li>
                    <li><strong>Verify surgeon credentials:</strong> Board certification, years of experience, before/after galleries</li>
                    <li><strong>Complete pre-operative testing:</strong> Blood work, EKG for patients over 40, imaging as needed</li>
                    <li><strong>Disclose complete medical history:</strong> Medications, supplements, previous surgeries, allergies</li>
                    <li><strong>Follow pre-operative instructions:</strong> Stop smoking (4-6 weeks before), avoid blood thinners</li>
                    <li><strong>Arrange proper aftercare:</strong> Stay near hospital for initial recovery period</li>
                    <li><strong>Budget for possible revision:</strong> Even with best surgeons, 5-10% may need adjustment</li>
                </ol>
            </div>

            <div class="warning-box">
                <h4>⚠️ Red Flags to Avoid</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>Extremely low prices that seem too good to be true</li>
                    <li>Surgeons who guarantee results or don't discuss risks</li>
                    <li>Facilities without proper accreditation or emergency equipment</li>
                    <li>Pressure tactics to book immediately</li>
                    <li>No before/after photos or unwillingness to provide references</li>
                    <li>Surgeons who are not board-certified in plastic surgery</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

